    {
      "id": 17592186049843,
      "mathAcademyId": 376,
      "title": "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Algebraic Expressions",
      "uuid": "18188755-d27b-5ea0-8e93-047aeb60e2d7"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186070019,
      "id": "6a92a024-3077-5b06-9926-573d76e002f6",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "When an equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1\\. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n2\\. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n3\\. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3.$ We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3,$ where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"",
      "mathAcademyId": "8692",
      "stepId": "17592186069617",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068475,
      "id": "e261bc60-5f18-5ce3-8444-d66fb71d6b56",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068725,
        "title": "Solving Equations With Integer Solutions"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve $2x^2 - 27=5.$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-13966",
      "stepId": "17592186069618",
      "title": "Solving Equations With Integer Solutions",
      "workedSolution": "First, we isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-27 & =5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\ |x| & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068476,
      "id": "f81f6359-676f-52fc-9e50-90a17ad8c71b",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068726,
        "title": "Solving Equations With Fractional Solutions"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve for $p$ where $4p^2 - 11 = 14.$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-13968",
      "stepId": "17592186069619",
      "title": "Solving Equations With Fractional Solutions",
      "workedSolution": "First, we isolate the $p^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{p}^{2}-11 & =14 \\\\ 4{p}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{p}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |p| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{25}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ |p| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ p & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068477,
      "id": "116d4e70-b784-54f1-b8a9-1880b475e2a2",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068727,
        "title": "Solving Equations With Radical Solutions"
      },
      "markdown": "Find $t$ if $2t^2 = 36.$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-13970",
      "stepId": "17592186069620",
      "title": "Solving Equations With Radical Solutions",
      "workedSolution": "First, we isolate the $t^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t2t^2 &= 36\\\\\n\t\tt^2 &= 18\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\t\t\n\t\t\\sqrt{t^2} &= \\sqrt{18} \\\\\n\t\t\\vert t \\vert &= \\sqrt{18} \\\\\n\t\t\\vert t \\vert &= \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n\t\t\\vert t \\vert &= \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\t\t\\vert t \\vert &= 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\t\tt &= \\pm 3\\sqrt{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
